  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Ganda celebrations are big, colourful and long. The kwanjula, where a couple is introduced to the bride’s family, is one of the most important.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Kwanjula eri ku Lwamukaaga.", "base": "The introduction is on Saturday." },
      { "target": "Ndeese ekirabo.", "base": "I have brought a gift." },
      { "target": "Kulika, mugole!", "base": "Congratulations, bride!" },
      { "target": "Tujaguze awamu!", "base": "Let’s celebrate together!" },
      { "target": "Nkwagaliza amazaalibwa amalungi!", "base": "Happy birthday!" },
      { "target": "Mwebale okujja!", "base": "Thank you for coming!" }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "kwanjula",
        "title": "The kwanjula",
        "body": [
          "At a **kwanjula**, the groom’s family visits the bride’s home with many gifts. A spokesperson speaks for each side, and the bride’s **ssenga** (paternal aunt) plays a central role, guiding her and presenting the groom.",
          "Women wear **gomesi** and men **kkanzu** (Unit 10). The day ends with food, music and dancing."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "wishes",
        "title": "Wishing well",
        "body": [
          "**Nkwagaliza** means “I wish you”: add what you wish: **Nkwagaliza amazaalibwa amalungi** (a good birthday). To a group, say **Mbaagaliza**. **Kulika** congratulates someone on something done: a wedding, a new baby, a journey."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Everyone contributes",
        "body": [
          "Weddings are often paid for together: friends and relatives hold planning meetings and pledge money. Being invited to a meeting means you are close to the family."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "stories": [
    {
      "id": "the-kwanjula",
      "title": "The introduction",
      "unlockAfter": "u28s3l3",
      "cast": ["nakato", "jjajja"],
      "summary": "Nakato gets ready for her cousin’s kwanjula with Jjajja.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "line": { "speaker": "jjajja", "target": "Nakato, oyambadde gomesi yo?", "base": "Nakato, have you put on your gomesi?" },
          "gloss": { "oyambadde": "you have put on" }
        },
        { "line": { "speaker": "nakato", "target": "Yee, Jjajja. Nnungi?", "base": "Yes, Grandma. Is it nice?" } },
        { "line": { "speaker": "jjajja", "target": "Nnungi nnyo! Ekirabo kiri wa?", "base": "Very nice! Where is the gift?" } },
        { "line": { "speaker": "nakato", "target": "Kiri wano. Sukaali n’omunnyo.", "base": "It’s here. Sugar and salt." } },
        { "question": "What gift is Nakato bringing?", "options": ["A cake", "Sugar and salt", "A hoe"], "answer": 1 },
        { "line": { "speaker": "jjajja", "target": "Kirungi. Ssenga ayamba omugole.", "base": "Good. The aunt is helping the bride." } },
        {
          "line": { "speaker": "nakato", "target": "Abagenyi bangi nnyo!", "base": "There are so many guests!" },
          "gloss": { "Abagenyi": "guests" }
        },
        { "line": { "speaker": "jjajja", "target": "Yee. Leero tujaguza.", "base": "Yes. Today we celebrate." } },
        { "question": "Who helps the bride at a kwanjula?", "options": ["The ssenga", "The Kabaka", "The teacher"], "answer": 0 },
        {
          "line": {
            "speaker": "nakato",
            "target": "Kulika, mugole! Nkwagaliza obulamu obulungi!",
            "base": "Congratulations, bride! I wish you a good life!"
          },
          "gloss": { "obulamu": "life" }
        },
        {
          "question": "How does Nakato say “I wish you”?",
          "options": ["Nkwagaliza", "Nkwagala", "Nkulinze"],
          "answer": 0,
          "optionsLang": "target"
        },
        {
          "line": { "speaker": "jjajja", "target": "Tujaguze! Mwebale okujja mwenna!", "base": "Let’s celebrate! Thank you all for coming!" },
          "gloss": { "mwenna": "all of you" }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
